CREATE concept:searching_moore_voting SET slug = 'searching_moore_voting', title = 'Moore Voting', category = 'searching', description = '# Moore Voting\n\n**Category:** `searching` | **Language Track:** Java (Interview Focus), Rust, Python, TypeScript\n\n---\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nboilerplate, because `==` isn\'t `const` yet\n\n### Original Rust Signatures\n```rust\npub fn moore_voting_2pass(arr: &[T]) -> Option<&T>;\n```\n\n### Complexity\n- **Time Complexity:** `O(N)`\n- **Space Complexity:** `O(1)`\n\n---\n\n## Java Interview Strategy & Tips\n\n- In Binary Search, ALWAYS calculate midpoint using `int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;` to avoid 32-', status = 'published';
CREATE implementation:searching_moore_voting_rust SET language = 'rust', source_code = '/*\n\n    Moore\'s voting algorithm finds out the strictly majority-occurring element\n    without using extra space\n    and O(n) + O(n) time complexity\n\n    It is built on the intuition that a strictly major element will always have a net occurrence as 1.\n    Say, array given: 9 1 8 1 1\n    Here, the algorithm will work as:\n\n    (for finding element present >(n/2) times)\n    (assumed: all elements are >0)\n\n    Initialisation: ele=0, cnt=0\n    Loop begins.\n\n    loop 1: arr[0]=9\n    ele = 9\n    cnt=1 (since cnt = 0, cnt increments to 1 and ele = 9)\n\n    loop 2: arr[1]=1\n    ele = 9\n    cnt= 0 (since in this turn of the loop, the array[i] != ele, cnt decrements by 1)\n\n    loop 3: arr[2]=8\n    ele = 8\n    cnt=1 (since cnt = 0, cnt increments to 1 and ele = 8)\n\n    loop 4: arr[3]=1\n    ele = 8\n    cnt= 0 (since in this turn of the loop, the array[i] != ele, cnt decrements by 1)\n\n    loop 5: arr[4]=1\n    ele = 9\n    cnt=1 (since cnt = 0, cnt increments to 1 and ele = 1)\n\n    Now, this ele should be the majority element if there\'s any\n    To check, a quick O(n) loop is run to check if the count of ele is >(n/2), n being the length of the array\n\n    -1 is returned when no such element is found.\n\n*/\n\n// boilerplate, because `==` isn\'t `const` yet\nconst fn eq_s(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> bool {\n    if a.len() != b.len() {\n        return false;\n    }\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] != b[i] {\n            return false;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    true\n}\n\npub fn moore_voting_2pass<T: Eq>(arr: &[T]) -> Option<&T> {\n    // =========================================================================\n    // 🎯 YOUR MISSION: IMPLEMENT THIS METHOD (moore_voting_2pass)!\n    // Follow the Socratic hints and questions in the comments above.\n    // =========================================================================\n    todo!("Implement moore_voting_2pass");\n}\n\npub const fn moore_voting_2pass_c<\'a>(arr: &[&\'a [u8]]) -> Option<&\'a [u8]> {\n    let n = arr.len();\n', ast_hash = 'seed_hash', commit_sha = 'HEAD', original_authors = ['TheAlgorithms Community'], keywords = ['searching', 'moore_voting'], license_spdx = 'MIT', origin_repo = 'repo:rust', origin_path = 'problems/searching/moore_voting';
